The rules of CDNF, written in CDNF: This file parses as valid cdnf.


:cdnf:

The Colon Dash and Newline Format is a natural text format designed to be computer parseable too.
A plain message is already a document, and its few marks are the
ones people write by hand anyway, colons, dashes and new lines.

Quotes are a last resort. Every rule here is made so that natural text
needs as few of them as possible.


::elements::

There are 3 types of elements on cdnf files:
- a text body
- a pair : what comes before a colon, and the element after it
- a list : items, each started by one or two dashes at the beginning of a line

CDNF has no data types other than text, and sections and elements are purely structural.


:::text bodies:::

A text body runs on until a blank line, or until a pair or a list item starts.
One newline inside a text body is part of it, so this body is
three lines.


::::plain text body examples::::

An ASCII table can also be written directly as a text body. Since its
lines begin with a pipe rather than a dash or a colon, CDNF treats the
whole table as an ordinary text element.

| name    | age | city   |
|---------|-----|--------|
| alice   | 25  | tokyo  |
| bob     | 39  | LA     |
| charlie | 19  | Paris  |

Times, links, paths, colors, negative numbers and headings are all plain text:
- a time : 12:54
- a link : https://example.com
- a path : C:\Windows\System32\
- a color : #ff8800
- a negative number : -5
- a heading : # Title


::::quoted text bodies::::

Where an element starts, a quote opens a quoted text body, running to the
next quote. Everything between is kept as written, colons, dashes,
comment marks and blank lines too. A backslash before a quote escapes it.

a quoted text body : "colons : and - dashes <:-> stay
as they are,

blank lines too, and \"quotes\" are kept by being escaped.
A backslash followed by anything but a quote is just a backslash, like C:\Windows\.
To keep a backslash right before a quote, write two, like \\" this.
A quoted text can't end in a backslash, so this one ends in a dot."

Outside quotes, a quote is plain text, like "this" in the middle of a text body.

A quoted text body can be followed by a colon that makes a pair, or by a newline.


::::when to quote::::

Only a text that would otherwise read as something else needs quotes, one that:
- holds a colon followed by a space or a line end
- starts a line with one or two dashes and a space
- starts a line with a colon
- holds a comment mark at a line's start or after a space
- starts with a quote
- has spaces at its start or end, or empty lines inside
- is empty


:::pairs:::

A colon makes a pair when spaces or a newline follow it, anywhere but at a line's start.
The pair is of what came before it and the next element after it, whatever element that is.
A colon followed by anything else is just text, like the ones in 12:54, http://example.com or C:\Windows\System32.

a pair : its right

a pair of a pair : left : right <:-> parses to ("a pair of a pair", (left, right))

a time : 12:54 <:-> parses to ("a time", "12:54")

name:value <:-> parses to the single text "name:value"

a pair of a list :
- the list's first item
- its last item

The right part of a pair can start after any number of spaces or newlines as follows.

left of a very long pair:



right of a very long pair



Note: A text body on the right side of a pair, followed by more text on the very next line forms a single text body. For example:

text body 1 : text
body 2 : text body 3 <:-> parses as (text body 1, (text\nbody 2, text body 3))

So pairs one after another are parted by a blank line, or written as list items.


:::lists:::

A list item is a line of any spaces, exactly one or two dashes, at least one space, and an element
starting on that same line, any element but a section header.
The list goes on while more items follow. Empty lines are valid between list items.

An item's element always starts on its dash's line, so lists nest only through a pair's colon.


::::dashes::::

Any other dashes at a line's start are just text, so these lines are one plain text body:
-5 degrees
--no space after the dashes
--- three dashes and a space
-

A dash anywhere but at a line's start is plain text, even right after a colon.

leftside: - rightside <:-> parses as ("leftside", "- rightside")

- - an item whose text starts with a dash <:-> parses as ["- an item whose text starts with a dash"]


::::ending lists::::

A list ends when any element other than a list item follows it.

A line right after an item's text, with no dashes, is more of that text, not a new element:
- an item whose text
runs on two lines

A list nested in another has its last item marked with 2 dashes instead, when the end of the nested list would be ambiguous otherwise.

nesting :
- an item
- an item holding a list :
    - an inner item
    -- the inner list's last item
- back in the outer list
-- two lists ending at once :
    - the inner list's one item


::sections::

:::headers:::

A line of a number of colons, a non empty text body without newlines, and the same number of colons again is a section header.

A section's header example in a pair : "::example section header ::"

A header's name can be a quoted text body, when it needs one : ":::\"a header: with a colon\":::"


:::levels:::

Sections can be recursively nested by using more colons. However only the necessary amount of colons may be used, example:
"
:first level:
:::third level???:::
"
is invalid because we lacked the level 2 header to reach level 3.

The first section of any document is always level 1: ":this is a level 1 header example, single colon:"


:::scope:::

Sections are greedy, anything after a section header is considered to be under that section.

A header closes all previous sections with equal or deeper level of nesting.

The elements before any section are the document's own and don't form part of any section.


::spacing and comments::

:::spacing:::

What counts as a space, a newline or an empty line is whatever a reader sees as one.
Tabs and any other blank characters are spaces, a carriage return before a newline is
part of that newline, and the invisible byte order mark some editors put at a file's start is nothing.

        Spaces at a text body line's start and end mean nothing, so a text body may be
        indented however it reads best.

Spaces around a dash or a colon mean nothing. Indentation is for human reader clarity alone.

Any run of empty lines, including lines of only spaces, is equivalent to 2 newlines.


:::comments:::

the comment mark : "<:->"

A comment mark opens a comment, running to its line's end, it means
nothing and is ignored. <:-> like this one

A comment mark opens a comment only at a line's start, or after at least one space.
Glued to the text before it, it is just text, like in this<:->that.

A hash is plain text, so colors and markdown headings need no quotes.

A comment after a colon counts as that line's end, so the colon still makes a pair,
its element starting on the next line.

items: <:-> the list below
- an item
- another item

Glued to the colon, the mark is text, and so is the colon, so items:<:-> is a single text.

A line holding only a comment is not considered a blank line or existent at all.
